Supreme People's Court

Several Opinions of the Supreme People's Court on Standardizing the Case-Filing for Retrial (Provisional Implementation)

Fa Fa [2002] No. 13

In order to strengthen trial supervision and standardize the case-filing for retrial, these Opinions are formulated in accordance with the relevant provisions of the Criminal Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China, the Civil Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China, and the Administrative Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China, and with reference to trial practice.

Article 1 If, after review, a people's court at any level or a higher people's court considers that a final judgment rendered by itself or by a lower people's court meets the conditions for case-filing for retrial, it shall decide or order a retrial.

Where a people's procuratorate files a protest in accordance with law against a final judgment rendered by a people's court, the case shall be filed for retrial.

Article 2 Local people's courts at all levels and special people's courts shall be responsible for the case-filing for retrial of the following cases:

(1) Final judgments rendered by themselves that meet the conditions for case-filing for retrial;

(2) Cases where a lower court, upon review, has dismissed the application or has altered the original judgment through retrial, and such cases meet the conditions for case-filing for retrial;

(3) Cases where a higher people's court has directed a retrial;

(4) Cases where a people's procuratorate has filed a protest in accordance with law.

Article 3 The Supreme People's Court shall be responsible for the case-filing for retrial of the following cases:

(1) Final judgments rendered by itself that meet the conditions for case-filing for retrial;

(2) Cases where a higher people's court, upon review, has dismissed the application or has altered the original judgment through retrial, and such cases meet the conditions for case-filing for retrial;

(3) Cases where the Supreme People's Procuratorate has filed a protest in accordance with law;

(4) Cases that the Supreme People's Court considers should be retried by itself.

Article 4 If a higher people's court considers it truly necessary, it may directly initiate a review of a final judgment rendered by a lower people's court. If, after review, it considers that the conditions for case-filing for retrial are met, it may decide or order a retrial.

Article 5 A person who applies for a retrial or files a petition with a people's court shall submit the following materials:

(1) A written application for retrial or a petition, which shall state the basic information of the parties concerned and the facts and grounds for the application for retrial or the petition;

(2) The original first-instance and second-instance judgments, rulings, and other legal documents. If the case has been reviewed or retried by a people's court, the notice of dismissal, the judgment or ruling on retrial shall be attached;

(3) If the application for retrial or the petition is based on the ground that new evidence proves that the facts found in the original judgment are indeed erroneous, a list of evidence, a list of witnesses, and copies or photographs of the main evidence shall be attached; if the investigation and collection of evidence by the people's court is required, a lead on evidence shall be attached.

If an application for retrial or a petition does not comply with the provisions of the preceding paragraph, the people's court shall not review it.

Article 6 An application for retrial or a petition shall generally be reviewed and handled by the people's court that rendered the final judgment.

If an application for retrial or a petition is submitted to a higher people's court without having been reviewed and handled by the people's court that rendered the final judgment, the higher people's court shall generally refer it to the court that rendered the final judgment for review; if the party persists in the application for retrial or petition after it has been reviewed and handled by the court that rendered the final judgment, the higher people's court shall accept it.

If an application for retrial or a petition is submitted directly to a higher-level people's court without having been reviewed and handled by the court that rendered the final judgment or by the next higher people's court, the higher-level people's court shall refer it to the next lower people's court for handling.

Article 7 With respect to a petition against a final criminal judgment, if any of the following circumstances exists, the people's court shall decide to conduct a retrial:

(1) Evidence that was not collected at the time of trial or was not admitted may overturn the original conviction and sentence;

(2) The main evidence is insufficient or lacks probative force;

(3) The main factual basis of the original judgment has been legally changed or revoked;

(4) The main evidence on which the conviction and sentence were based is self-contradictory;

(5) The legal provisions cited are erroneous or ineffective laws have been applied in violation of Article 12 of the Criminal Law;

(6) The provisions on the retroactive effect of the law have been violated;

(7) The sentence is clearly improper;

(8) The trial procedure was illegal, affecting the fair adjudication of the case;

(9) Judicial personnel, during the trial of the case, solicited or accepted bribes, engaged in favoritism and malpractice, and thereby rendered a judgment contrary to law.

Article 8 With respect to an application for retrial against a final civil judgment or mediation, if any of the following circumstances exists, the people's court shall order a retrial:

(1) There is evidence that the applicant for retrial was previously unaware of or was unable to provide, and such evidence may overturn the original judgment;

(2) The main evidence is insufficient or lacks probative force;

(3) The main factual basis of the original judgment has been legally changed or revoked;

(4) For the same legal fact or legal relationship, there exist two conflicting effective legal instruments, and the applicant challenges the later effective legal instrument;

(5) The legal provisions cited are erroneous or laws that are ineffective or not yet effective have been applied;

(6) The provisions on the retroactive effect of the law have been violated;

(7) The mediation agreement clearly violates the principle of voluntariness, its content violates the law, or it harms the interests of the state, the public interest, or the interests of others;

(8) The trial procedure was illegal, affecting the fair adjudication of the case;

(9) Judicial personnel, during the trial of the case, solicited or accepted bribes, engaged in favoritism and malpractice, and thereby rendered a judgment contrary to law.

Article 9 With respect to a petition against a final administrative judgment, if any of the following circumstances exists, the people's court shall order a retrial:

(1) The case should have been accepted in accordance with law but was not accepted or the lawsuit was dismissed;

(2) New evidence may alter the original judgment;

(3) The main evidence is insufficient or lacks probative force;

(4) The main factual basis of the original judgment has been legally changed or revoked;

(5) The legal provisions cited are erroneous or laws that are ineffective or not yet effective have been applied;

(6) The provisions on the retroactive effect of the law have been violated;

(7) The administrative compensation mediation agreement violates the principle of voluntariness, its content violates the law, or it harms the interests of the state, the public interest, or the interests of others;

(8) The trial procedure was illegal, affecting the fair adjudication of the case;

(9) Judicial personnel, during the trial of the case, solicited or accepted bribes, engaged in favoritism and malpractice, and thereby rendered a judgment contrary to law.

Article 10 A people's court shall accept a petition filed by a petitioner in a criminal case within two years after the execution of the penalty is completed. If the petition is filed after two years, it shall be accepted if any of the following circumstances exists:

(1) The original defendant may be declared not guilty;

(2) The original defendant filed a petition with the people's court within the period specified in this Article, but the people's court did not accept it;

(3) The case is difficult, complex, or major.

If the conditions in the preceding paragraph are not met, the people's court shall not accept the petition.

Article 11 If, in a criminal case with a civil action attached, a petition is filed only concerning the civil part, the people's court shall generally not file the case for retrial, except where there is evidence that the civil part is clearly improper and the original defendant has the ability to compensate.

Article 12 A people's court shall not accept an application for retrial or a petition filed by an applicant or petitioner in a civil or administrative case more than two years after the judgment or ruling becomes final.

Article 13 A people's court shall not accept an application for retrial or a petition filed by a person who does not have the legal standing as a party.

Article 14 A people's court shall not accept an application for retrial in the following civil cases:

(1) Cases tried by a people's court in accordance with the supervision procedure, the public summons procedure, or the bankruptcy and debt repayment procedure;

(2) Cases where a people's court has ruled to set aside an arbitration award or has ruled not to enforce an arbitration award;

(3) Cases where a people's court has dissolved a marriage by judgment or mediation, except where a party applies for retrial concerning the division of property.

Article 15 A higher people's court shall generally not accept an application for retrial or a petition if the case has been upheld after being retried by the court one level above the court that rendered the final judgment under the trial supervision procedure, or if the case has been dismissed after review by two levels of people's courts under the trial supervision procedure.

However, this does not apply if the applicant or petitioner raises new grounds that meet the conditions set forth in Article 204 of the Criminal Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China, Article 179 of the Civil Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China, Article 62 of the Administrative Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China, and Articles 7, 8, and 9 of these Opinions, or if the original defendant in a criminal case may be declared not guilty.

Article 16 If, after a retrial by or a review dismissal by the Supreme People's Court, the applicant for retrial or the petitioner still disagrees and files an application for retrial or a petition, it shall not be accepted.

Article 17 These Opinions shall come into force as of November 1, 2002. Where previous provisions on case-filing for retrial are inconsistent with these Opinions, these Opinions shall prevail.

September 10, 2002
